<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Zancle</h1><p class="st-meta">730 BCE – 288 BCE (approx.) · capital Zancle</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Greek city on the Strait of Messina, named after its sickle-shaped harbour and renamed Messana in the 5th century BCE, which controlled the passage between Sicily and Italy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Settlers from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cumae">Cumae</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chalcis">Chalcis</a> founded Zancle in the second half of the 8th century BCE, at first as a base of '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>', on the sickle-shaped peninsula that gave the city its name (zanklon, sickle). Facing <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rhegion">Rhegion</a> across the Strait, it commanded one of the most important sea passages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Zancle founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mylae">Mylae</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/himera">Himera</a> and grew rich on traffic through the Strait. Around 490 BCE refugees from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/samos">Samos</a> seized it, and soon afterwards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anaxilas">Anaxilas</a>, tyrant of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rhegion">Rhegion</a>, took it, settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messenians">Messenians</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> and renamed it <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mamertine-messana">Messana</a>. It was later a democracy, sided at times with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a>, and was destroyed by the Carthaginian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himilco">Himilco</a> in 396 BCE before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysius-i-of-syracuse">Dionysius I</a> refounded it.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 288 BCE Campanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mamertine-messana">Mamertines</a>, seized Messana and killed or expelled many citizens. Their appeal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 264 BCE triggered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a>. The city became Roman Messana and is today <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/zancle.jpg" alt="Zancle" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Hoernigk · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Messina_2003.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>730 BCE – 288 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zancle</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Anaxilas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Italy</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Messana (Greek), Messene</dd></dl></aside></div>
